STEP 5 -- STATIC INTER-VRF ROUTING  (tests G5)
------------------------------------------------------------
Objective: the guide's own escape hatch -- deliberate, route-by-route communication between two
VRFs -- works, and only for what it names.

  Applied 23:29:02 (both accepted with no message):
    awplus(config)# ip route vrf red  192.168.48.0/24 vlan48
    awplus(config)# ip route vrf blue 192.168.10.0/24 vlan10
  Note the form actually accepted here is **network + EGRESS VLAN, with no next-hop address**.
  The guide's p.17 text says "These static routes must specify both the egress VLAN and next
  hop IP address", while its own worked example on the same page uses the VLAN-only form
  (`ip route vrf red 192.168.20.0/24 vlan10`).  The VLAN-only form is what this build accepts
  and what works; the prose overstates the requirement.

    awplus# show ip route vrf red
    C       192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10
    **S       192.168.48.0/24 [1/0] is directly connected, vlan48**
    awplus# show ip route vrf blue
    **S       192.168.10.0/24 [1/0] is directly connected, vlan10**
    C       192.168.48.0/24 is directly connected, vlan48
    awplus# show running-config | include ip route
    ip route vrf red 192.168.48.0/24 vlan48
    ip route vrf blue 192.168.10.0/24 vlan10
  A static route in one VRF whose egress interface lives in another -- rendered as
  "is directly connected, <the other VRF's vlan>".

  Leg V5-inter-vrf-static.  cap eth3: 32 frames.  cap **eth4: 7 frames**
    192.168.48.2 (SUBJECT, on eth4) : 5 sent, **5 ARRIVED**        (0 in V4, immediately before)
    192.168.48.1 (SUBJECT-2)        : 5 sent, **5 echo replies**   (0 in V4)
    203.0.113.9  (CONTROL)          : 5 sent, **0** -- the leak is route-specific; isolation did
                                      not collapse, only the two named prefixes crossed
    192.168.10.1 (INSTRUMENT)       : 5 sent, 5 echo replies

  ⇒ **G5 CONFIRMED on the wire**, with the control proving the opening is exactly as wide as
